    "case_id": "461",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 70s presented with a persistent eruption of 5 years' duration. He had been treated with oral methotrexate for presumed chronic dermatitis after treatments with oral antibiotics, oral doxepin, and UV-B phototherapy had failed. Investigation of medical records revealed a history of HSV-2 infection on the buttocks prior to the development of the current eruption at the same site.\n\nMultiple erythematous and violaceous plaques and nodules clustered on the superior buttocks and intergluteal cleft bilaterally. The lesions were smooth and firm.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Syphilis screen results were negative. Molecular analysis of the T-cell receptor gamma gene showed a weak clonal band in a polyclonal background. Repeat molecular studies showed no evidence of clonal rearrangement.- Pathological findings:  First biopsy: Dense infiltrate of atypical CD4+ small to medium-sized lymphocytes in the upper dermis admixed with eosinophils and plasma cells. Epidermotropism was not identified.  Second biopsy: Similar to prior findings, but with more dense and atypical infiltrate showing epidermotropism.- Images:  Figure 1 Title: Clinical Presentation  Figure 1 Description: Multiple smooth, firm plaques and nodules on the superior buttocks and intergluteal cleft.    Figure 2 Title: Histopathological Findings  Figure 2A Description: Scanning view shows dense infiltrate filling the papillary dermis and extending into the reticular dermis  Figure 2B Description: Higher power shows dense, atypical infiltrate of small to medium-sized lymphocytes",
